# base.list -- the minimum COHERENT: COHERENT 0.7.3's own root set, plus what
# this system adds to it.
#
# WHAT DEFINES THE SET is the release itself rather than an opinion.  0.7.3 lays
# its root filesystem from /kit/hdproto, the mkproto(1) prototype carried on
# distribution volume 1, and that prototype IS the release's answer to what a
# machine needs before the other volumes arrive: the /bin and /etc it can be
# installed, checked and repaired with, the shell, and the console.  c900oses
# PLAN.md finding F-24 establishes that set off the three surviving release
# floppies, and F-24a maps it onto this list.  Everything hdproto names and this
# repository builds is here.
#
# WHAT HDPROTO NAMES AND THIS LIST DOES NOT, each for its own reason.  The
# console driver is a per-dist choice, so it comes from lists/console-*.list,
# which every distribution includes; /etc/ttys and /etc/profile likewise belong
# to the login and command components that read them.  db(1) is a deliberate
# divergence and not an oversight: 0.7.3 carried the symbolic debugger in its
# root set, and a component is named for what it IS, so a debugger ships with
# the development tools -- lists/toolchain.list stages it, built from this
# repository's base/cmd/db.  It is not missing and must not be restored here.
# /etc/swap is the only program left out altogether: 0.7.3's is the userland
# SWAPPER PROCESS init(8) execs through argv[1], and this system's init is
# compiled NOSWAPPER (base/cmd/init.c), so nothing would exec it.  Kernel
# swapping is a separate and live mechanism -- swapdev, swapio() and sdalloc()
# in the kernel, and the media descriptors' swapbot/swaptop -- and does not
# want a program here.  The rest of what hdproto creates and this list does not
# -- /etc/boottime, /etc/cpsys, /etc/pro.def -- is install-time state, written
# on the machine, not shipped.
#
# WHAT THIS SYSTEM ADDS is ten files 0.7.3 shipped on volumes 2 and 3 instead of
# in the root: date, expr, make, test, time and wc, because this machine is
# driven by a Makefile and a compile's cost has to be readable off the console,
# and /etc/{logmsg,motd,termcap,update}, because login(1), rc and the console
# read them.
#
# EVERY DISTRIBUTION STARTS HERE and adds to it.  Anything else -- the
# general-purpose command set (lists/runtime.list), the further editors, the
# networking, the window system, a compiler -- is a component a distribution
# includes by name.  What is on the disk is what somebody asked for.
#
# THE COMPILER IS NOT HERE.  Producing a program is not part of a runtime, and
# the two components that do it are the toolchain repository's artifacts:
# lists/toolchain.list installs `ours', the cc0/cc1/cc2 self-host fixpoint, and
# lists/toolchain-1985.list the recovered Mark Williams chain.  A guest that
# compiles without either takes a compiler ENVIRONMENT mapped in from the host
# as a mounted directory (the toolchain's docs/ENVIRONMENTS.md), and
# os/tests/buildenv rests on the image itself having none -- a compiler here
# would make that harness's negative control vacuous.
#
# NOR IS ANY OF THE HOSTFS MACHINERY.  Under the emulator a host directory is
# reached with the ordinary mount(2) on the shipped /dev/fd1 node: the medium
# is rendered on the host and attached with --floppy, and no guest driver is
# involved.  lists/hostfs.list carries /drv/hostfs, the mailbox block driver a
# host program serves, and a dist that includes it takes on the standing
# obligation to relink /drv/hostfs after every kernel relink.
#
# --- directory skeleton -----------------------------------------------------
# /usr is a directory on a single-filesystem medium and a mount point on a
# multi-filesystem one; either way an installed environment's headers stay
# visible after /etc/rc runs.
# PACKAGE (os/dist/PACKAGE-FORMAT).  A list with a `package' line is an
# addressable unit of a release channel; this is the one every distribution
# has, which is what `role base' says.
package  base
role     base
# KINDS -- see os/dist/PACKAGE-FORMAT.  `bin' is the list below; `src' is the
# complete corresponding source for every executable it installs and `man' the
# pages for those programs.  Both refuse to be cut from a guess.
kinds    src man

d	/bin
d	/dev
d	/drv
d	/etc
d	/lib
d	/mnt
d	/tmp	777 0 1
d	/usr
d	/usr/bin
d	/usr/include
d	/usr/lib
d	/usr/src

# --- /etc configuration -----------------------------------------------------
# rc mounts what the media declares, clears /tmp, and starts update; it also
# runs /bin/test and date, which is why both are in the command set.
f	/etc/rc
f	/etc/passwd
f	/etc/group
# login(1) prints /etc/logmsg and /etc/motd.
f	/etc/logmsg
f	/etc/motd
# TERM for the console; .profile's probe of /etc/console needs grep.
f	/.profile
f	/etc/termcap
e	/etc/mnttab	644 0 1
e	/etc/utmp	666 0 1

# --- coming up multi-user ---------------------------------------------------
# init reads /etc/ttys (lists/login-text.list) and spawns /etc/getty, which
# execs /bin/login, which execs the shell.  init also loads the console driver
# vidsel named, with /etc/load.
f	/etc/init
f	/etc/getty
f	/bin/login	4755 0 1
# login locks the terminal it is about to hand over by creating an LCK.. file
# in /usr/spool/uucp (base/cmd/login/lock.c), the same lock UUCP takes on a
# line, so the directory is part of coming up multi-user and not part of the
# spooler: without it login prints "cannot lock terminal" and refuses every
# login, on every console.
d	/usr/spool
d	/usr/spool/uucp
f	/bin/sh
f	/etc/load
# uload(1) is /etc/load's other half: it takes a loaded driver back out, which
# is what makes swapping a console or a device driver on a running machine
# possible without a reboot.
f	/etc/uload
f	/etc/update

# --- reaching the host's directory ------------------------------------------
# The whole transport, guest side: mount the rendered medium, umount it when the
# build is done so the host can extract what was written.  sync(1) is what makes
# a mid-run snapshot safe to extract.
f	/etc/mount
f	/etc/umount
f	/bin/sync

# --- building ---------------------------------------------------------------
# make(1) is the point of the exercise; it forks the shell for every command,
# so it needs the shell more than it needs anything else.
f	/bin/make
# ITS TWO LIBRARY FILES, WITHOUT WHICH IT DOES NOT START.  make(1) reads
# makemacros (default macros) and makeactions (the built-in suffix rules) from
# LIBPATH -- "/lib:/usr/lib" -- before it looks at a Makefile (cmd/make/make.c
# inlib(), make.h MACROFILE/ACTIONFILE), and without makemacros it exits with
# "make: cannot open makemacros" whatever the Makefile says.
f	/usr/lib/makemacros	644 0 1	src=dist/files/usr/lib/makemacros
f	/usr/lib/makeactions	644 0 1	src=dist/files/usr/lib/makeactions

# --- filesystems: making one, checking one, repairing one -------------------
# THIS IS WHY THE SET LOOKS LIKE THIS.  0.7.3's root filesystem is laid by
# mkfs(1) from the /kit/hdproto prototype, so the release put the tools that
# make and repair a filesystem in the root set: a machine that can only mount
# what somebody else made cannot install itself, cannot answer for a disc that
# came up FSDIRTY, and cannot bring a second volume into service.
#
#   mkfs     writes a filesystem, from a size or from a prototype.
#   mkproto  writes such a prototype out of an existing directory tree, which
#            is the other end of the same operation.
#   mknod    makes the device nodes a filesystem is reached through; the kernel
#            gives no unprivileged path to one (see the privileged file
#            utilities below).
#   check    the file-system check: the program that answers for a dirty disc
#            and writes FSCLEAN back into the super block.
#   icheck   the block-allocation half of it, dcheck the directory half; each
#            is usable on its own when check's report needs narrowing.
#   clri     clears an inode outright, which is the repair of last resort for
#            damage check cannot describe.
#   bad      maps out blocks the medium has lost, so a filesystem can be made
#            that does not use them.
#   dd       copies raw blocks with an explicit block size, which is how a
#            filesystem, a boot block or a whole volume is moved at all.
#   fdformat writes the sector headers a blank floppy has none of, which is the
#            step before mkfs can touch one at all.
f	/etc/fdformat
f	/etc/mkfs
f	/etc/mkproto
f	/etc/mknod
f	/bin/check
f	/bin/icheck
f	/bin/dcheck
f	/etc/clri
f	/bin/bad
f	/bin/dd

# --- the running system -----------------------------------------------------
# What a person at the console asks and answers with: which processes exist,
# which terminal this is, and how to stop something that will not stop.
# ps IS setuid root, the same bit the reference disk carries at /bin/ps 4755.
# The three files it opens -- /dev/kmem, /dev/mem and
# /dev/swap (cmd-3.x/ps.c) -- are 600 root nodes in dist/devices/hd.devices, so
# without the bit no ordinary user could run ps at all.
#
# THE TRADE, stated plainly, because it used to be made the other way: those
# nodes were 666 and ps was left 755, and 666 on /dev/rhd0 means any shell
# becomes root by writing /etc/passwd through the raw disk -- no su, no
# password, no setuid program needed.  The nodes are shut and the programs
# that legitimately read them carry the bit instead.  ps opens all
# three descriptors in execute() and then does setuid(getuid()) -- the same
# "Turn off setuid privileges" step cmd/write.c makes -- so the process-table
# walk, fttys() and getpwuid() all run as the real user.  -c and -k, which let
# the caller substitute the namelist and the memory file, drop it BEFORE the
# first open instead, so ps cannot be made to read a file its caller may not.
f	/bin/ps		4755 0 1
f	/bin/kill
f	/bin/tty

# --- editors ----------------------------------------------------------------
# 0.7.3's root set carried an editor, and this one carries three: a system that
# cannot edit /etc/rc after it comes up single-user is a system somebody has to
# rebuild the disc for.  All three read a termcap entry for $TERM, which is
# /etc/termcap above.
#
#   ed     the line editor, which needs no terminal description to work at all
#          and is therefore the one that always works.
#   me     MicroEMACS (os/cmd/me), the screen editor COHERENT provides;
#          lists/editors.list stages `emacs' as its second name.
#   elvis  the vi implementation.
f	/bin/ed
f	/usr/bin/me
f	/usr/bin/elvis
# elvis dispatches on the last character of argv[0] (cmd/elvis/main.c): `x'
# selects ex mode, `w' selects view (read-only), anything else -- `vi',
# `elvis' -- selects vi.  `ex', `view' and `vi' are also the names the
# Lexicon's own pages tell a user to type, and the ones everybody types
# regardless.  No symbolic links exist on this filesystem, so these are hard
# links to the one binary: one inode, link count 4.
l	/usr/bin/ex	to=/usr/bin/elvis
l	/usr/bin/view	to=/usr/bin/elvis
l	/usr/bin/vi	to=/usr/bin/elvis

# --- file utilities ---------------------------------------------------------
# What a Makefile, a person driving one, and an administrator reading a disc
# actually invoke.  Deliberately NOT here: sed, awk, sort, tr -- a Makefile
# that needs them wants lists/runtime.list, and adding it to a copy of the dist
# is one line.
f	/bin/ls
f	/bin/lc
f	/bin/cat
f	/bin/cp
# cpdir(1) copies a whole tree, which is how a system is installed from a
# volume and how one is moved to another disc.
f	/bin/cpdir
# THE PRIVILEGED FILE UTILITIES -- mkdir, rmdir, rm and mv.  All four are setuid
# root because this kernel gives a directory no unprivileged path in or out:
# umknod() refuses any type but IFPIPE unless super() (sys/coh/sys2.c), so
# mkdir(1) -- which has no mkdir(2) to call and builds the directory with
# mknod(dir, IFDIR|0777, 0) plus two links -- cannot make one; iucheck() refuses
# to unlink an IFDIR unless super() (sys/coh/fs1.c), so rmdir(1) and `rm -r'
# cannot take one away; and ulink() refuses to link an IFDIR unless super()
# (sys/coh/sys2.c), so `mv' cannot move one.  Each program polices itself with
# access(2) on the REAL uid before it uses the privilege (cmd/mkdir.c's
# access(parent, 03) and its chown to the caller, cmd/rm.c's ADEL walk,
# cmd/rmdir.c, cmd/mv.c's myuid/ADEL checks), which is what the setuid bit was
# designed around and why the reference disk carries it on all four.
# Without the bit a non-root user gets mkdir(1) printing "not the super-user"
# and rm/rmdir/mv failing on every directory.
f	/bin/mv		4755 0 1
f	/bin/rm		4755 0 1
f	/bin/ln
f	/bin/mkdir	4755 0 1
f	/bin/rmdir	4755 0 1
# The three that set what access(2) then tests.  chgrp and chown are here for
# the same reason mknod is: ownership on a freshly made filesystem is whatever
# made it, and nothing else can correct that.
f	/bin/chmod
f	/bin/chgrp
f	/bin/chown
f	/bin/pwd
f	/bin/echo
f	/bin/test
# The Lexicon's test(1) page: "Under COHERENT, the command `[' is linked to
# test.  If invoked as `[', test requires a closing `]'."  base/cmd/test.c
# takes that name from argv[0], and cmd/sh/exec2.c:126 execve()s the resolved
# path with the word as typed, so the second name reaches it intact.  The
# sweep stages a second copy of the same bytes under the second name, not an
# `l' hard link: the list format has no link type.
f	/bin/[		755 0 1
f	/bin/expr
f	/bin/find
f	/bin/grep
f	/bin/egrep
f	/bin/cmp
f	/bin/diff
# file(1) says what a thing is before anything tries to read it; od and scat
# are how a file no program understands gets read anyway -- od dumps it in any
# radix, scat prints it with the unprintable characters made visible.
f	/bin/file
f	/bin/od
f	/bin/scat
f	/bin/wc
f	/bin/df
f	/bin/du
# date and time are the instruments: a guest compile's cost is a number
# somebody has to be able to read off the console.
f	/bin/date
f	/bin/time
# who is here and not in the runtime fragment because the passwd file this
# list ships names it: the `who' account runs /bin/who as its login program,
# and login refuses an account whose program the image does not carry.
f	/bin/who

# --- the /bin versus /usr/bin split -----------------------------------------
# /usr is a separate filesystem that only /etc/rc mounts, so nothing in
# /usr/bin exists in the single-user maintenance shell -- and that is NORMAL,
# not a defect.  A root filesystem carrying only the basics, with ed(1) as the
# editor and no pager, is how Unix is supposed to look in single user.  Do not
# move a command to /bin to make single user more capable, and do not put its
# data files on the root filesystem for that reason either; help text belongs
# in /usr/lib beside more.help.
#
# The one reason that could justify moving something across, and how to act on
# it: space.  /usr is sized in dist/media/*.media, and the partition table is
# generated from those files into kboot.cfg (hostbuild/dist.py wdslots), so
# growing it is one edit.  On the 21 MB media /usr and /usr/man jointly own
# blocks 10336..31007 and the boundary between them sits where it does because
# man is read-only and sized to its content, leaving the growth room on the
# /usr side; the 42 MB development media gives /usr the whole tail of the
# drive.  Move a boundary rather than scatter commands across filesystems, and
# check the `mkimage' block counts before adding to either side.

# end of base.list
